    abstract:
      '<p class = "title"><strong>Feasibility and Stability of PWFA Injection Schemes in Hybrid Plasma Accelerators</strong></p>\n    <div class="authors-block">\n  <p class="authors">Edgar Anton Hartmann<sup>1</sup>, Ahmad Fahim Habib<sup>2</sup>, Antonio Tarzikhan<sup>1</sup>, Bernhard Hidding<sup>3</sup>, Mirela Cerchez<sup>4</sup>, Natascha Thomas<sup>1</sup>, Onur Bilen<sup>1</sup>, Thomas Heinemann<sup>1</sup></p>\n<ol class="affiliations">\n  <li>Heinrich Heine University D\u00fcsseldorf</li>\n  <li>University of Strathclyde</li>\n  <li>Heinrich Heine University D\u00fcsseldorf / University of Strathclyde / The Cockcroft Institute</li>\n  <li>Heinrich Heine University, D\u00fcsseldorf</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n    <div class = "abstract" style = "margin-top: 10px;">\n    <p>Hybrid plasma wakefield accelerators combine a laser wakefield accelerator (LWFA) stage with a particle-driven plasma wakefield accelerator (PWFA) stage, leveraging the complementary strengths of both approaches. The PWFA stage offers a robust way to improve the energy, stability, and beam quality of electron bunches produced by the LWFA. This makes the hybrid scheme a promising pathway for becoming a compact source for high-quality electron beams that can drive future light sources, such as free electron lasers. A key challenge in such systems is the sensitivity of the PWFA injection process to intrinsic jitters in the LWFA output. Using particle-in-cell simulations, we systematically investigate different PWFA injection schemes and characterize their stability in response to realistic jitter conditions of LWFA-produced electron bunches. Our results provide guidance for the design of robust hybrid accelerator configurations.</p>\n    </div>\n    ',
