    abstract:
      '<p class = "title"><strong>Recent developments of a laser-driven ion acceleration beamline at SIOM</strong></p>\n    <div class="authors-block">\n  <p class="authors">Jianhui Bin<sup>1</sup>, Wenjie Peng<sup>1</sup>, Junfeng Chen<sup>1</sup>, Make Zhao<sup>1</sup>, Jianzhou Zhuang<sup>1</sup>, Haofan Sun<sup>1</sup>, Haoran Wang<sup>1</sup>, Luolin Zeng<sup>1</sup>, Xihan Yang<sup>1</sup>, Haotian Hu<sup>1</sup>, Xinxin Yan<sup>1</sup>, Fen Li<sup>1</sup>, Shuyin Zheng<sup>1</sup>, Minghong Zhong<sup>1</sup>, Yu Zhang<sup>1</sup>, Yi Xu<sup>1</sup>, Yuxin Leng<sup>1</sup>, Ruxin Li<sup>1</sup></p>\n<ol class="affiliations">\n  <li>Shanghai Institute of Optics and Fine Mechanics, Chinese Academy of Sciences</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n    <div class = "abstract" style = "margin-top: 10px;">\n    <p>Laser driven ion acceleration provides a route to achieve high quality ion beams, which could be superior for specific applications. In this talk I will present recent development of a laser driven ion acceleration beam line based on a homemade table-top 200 TW laser system at Shanghai Institute of Optics and Fine Mechanics (SIOM). Our major motivation is the potential application of such pulsed ion sources.</p>\n    </div>\n    ',
